<!-- ── SECTION 01 ── -->
<section id="s-bottleneck" data-code="bottleneck">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Bottleneck</div>
<h2>Sequential Decoding: The GPU Utilization Crisis</h2>
<p>Modern autoregressive LLMs generate one token at a time — each step requires a full forward pass through billions of parameters, waits for the result, then starts the next. This seriality kills throughput: the GPU spends most of its time waiting for memory bandwidth, not computing.</p>
<p>For a 1-trillion-parameter Mixture-of-Experts model like MiMo 2.5 Pro, this is catastrophic. Each token fetch from GPU memory takes microseconds; modern H200s and B200s can execute thousands of FLOPS in that time but sit idle waiting for the next token's KV cache to arrive. The result is less than 5% GPU compute utilization during decode.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">decode-bottleneck.canvas — autoregressive vs parallel</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click to toggle</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body" style="min-height:200px">
    <canvas id="bottleneck-canvas" style="width:100%;height:190px;display:block;border-radius:6px"></canvas>
    <div style="display:flex;gap:.75rem;margin-top:.75rem;justify-content:center">
      <button id="btn-ar" class="step-nav" style="padding:.3rem .9rem;border-radius:5px;font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);cursor:pointer;border:1px solid var(--border-hi);background:var(--surface);color:var(--muted2)">Autoregressive</button>
      <button id="btn-block" class="step-nav" style="padding:.3rem .9rem;border-radius:5px;font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);cursor:pointer;border:1px solid rgba(139,92,246,.5);background:var(--purple-dim);color:var(--purple-l)">Block Parallel</button>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The autoregressive bottleneck is the primary motivation behind speculative decoding: can we hide the serial latency behind a smarter algorithm?</p>

<div class="compare-grid">
  <div class="compare-card bad">
    <div class="compare-label">❌ Autoregressive — serial</div>
    <div class="compare-desc">Each token requires a complete forward pass. GPU utilization: &lt;5%. Latency grows linearly with sequence length.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="compare-card good">
    <div class="compare-label">✓ Block Parallel — concurrent</div>
    <div class="compare-desc">An entire block of 8–16 tokens is denoised in one forward pass. GPU utilization: &gt;60%. Latency is roughly constant per block.</div>
  </div>
</div>
</section>

<!-- ── SECTION 02 ── -->
<section id="s-speculative" data-code="speculative">
<div class="section-no">02 — Speculative Decoding</div>
<h2>Draft-and-Verify: The Key to Lossless Speedup</h2>
<p>Speculative decoding exploits a key asymmetry: <em>verifying</em> a sequence of tokens is much faster than <em>generating</em> them one by one. A large target model can verify a batch of γ proposed tokens in a single forward pass — the same cost as generating just one token autoregressively.</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Speedup formula</div>
  L = (T<span class="math-var">draft</span> + T<span class="math-var">verify</span>) / τ
  <br>
  <span class="math-comment">// τ = expected accepted tokens per cycle (incl. bonus token)</span>
  <br>
  <span class="math-comment">// Speedup = L_target / L = τ × L_target / (T_draft + T_verify)</span>
</div>

<p>This formula crystallizes the design tension: to maximize speedup, you need a <strong>high acceptance length τ</strong> (the draft model is accurate) and a <strong>low drafting cost T<sub>draft</sub></strong> (the draft model is fast). These goals usually conflict — more capable draft models are slower to run.</p>

<h3>Why EAGLE-3 hits a ceiling</h3>
<p>State-of-the-art autoregressive drafters like EAGLE-3 use a single transformer layer fed with hidden features from the target model. Even with tree-based speculation (generating a tree of 60 candidate tokens), they face a hard ceiling:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Autoregressive drafting is sequential — generating γ=16 tokens requires 16 forward passes through the draft model</li>
  <li>Drafting cost T<sub>draft</sub> = γ × t<sub>step</sub> grows linearly with budget γ</li>
  <li>A shallow 1-layer drafter is too weak to predict far ahead; adding layers increases latency proportionally</li>
  <li>In practice, EAGLE-3 achieves 1.7–2.3× speedup on Qwen3-8B at temperature=0</li>
</ul>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">drafting-cost.canvas — latency vs draft budget γ</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">autoregressive grows linearly; diffusion stays flat</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="cost-canvas" style="width:100%;height:220px;display:block;border-radius:6px"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>Diffusion drafters break this ceiling. Because they generate all γ tokens in <strong>one parallel forward pass</strong>, T<sub>draft</sub> = t<sub>parallel</sub> is essentially constant regardless of block size. This unlocks a new design space: a deeper, more capable draft model can be built without increasing latency.</p>
</section>

<!-- ── SECTION 03 ── -->
<section id="s-dflash" data-code="dflash">
<div class="section-no">03 — DFlash Architecture</div>
<h2>The Target Knows Best: KV Injection of Context Features</h2>
<p>The naive approach — just swap a diffusion model in as the drafter — fails badly. Without access to the target model's internal representations, the drafter must predict future tokens from scratch, yielding only 2–3× speedup.</p>
<p>DFlash's key insight, borrowed from the EAGLE line of work: <strong>large autoregressive LLMs' hidden features implicitly encode information about multiple future tokens</strong>. Instead of ignoring this signal, DFlash fuses it directly into the diffusion drafter's attention computation.</p>

