<p>The architectural insight: instead of feeding hidden features as <em>input tokens</em> to the draft model (EAGLE's approach, where the signal dilutes as depth increases), DFlash treats them as <strong>persistent Key-Value pairs injected into every draft layer's attention</strong>. The target context is always present throughout the entire draft stack.</p>

<h3>Why KV injection beats input conditioning</h3>
<p>In EAGLE, hidden features are concatenated with token embeddings at the input. By layer 4 of the draft model, the original target signal has been mixed and diluted — adding more layers yields diminishing returns. In DFlash, the projected target features are stored in the KV cache and accessed at full fidelity by every layer's attention heads. Acceptance length scales consistently with draft model depth.</p>
</section>

<!-- ── SECTION 04 ── -->
<section id="s-block" data-code="block">
<div class="section-no">04 — Block Diffusion Drafting</div>
<h2>Filling the Mask: Parallel Token Generation in One Pass</h2>
<p>Standard draft models generate tokens left-to-right: predict token 1, then token 2, then token 3… Each step depends on the previous. DFlash replaces this with <strong>block diffusion</strong>: given an anchor token (the last accepted clean token), simultaneously predict the next γ masked positions in a single forward pass.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-title">🔑 The key shift</div>
  <p>Autoregressive drafting: T<sub>draft</sub> = γ × t<sub>step</sub> — grows linearly.<br>
  Diffusion drafting: T<sub>draft</sub> = t<sub>parallel</sub> — roughly constant regardless of γ.<br>
  For γ=16 tokens, DFlash's 5-layer drafter is <strong>faster</strong> than EAGLE-3's 1-layer drafter.</p>
</div>

