<p>In the MiMo 2.5 Pro deployment, coding tasks achieve an average acceptance length of <strong>6.30 out of a block size of 8</strong> — meaning roughly 6–7 of every 8 draft tokens are accepted per verification round. This translates directly to the observed speedup: each verification pass "pays for" 6× more tokens than a purely autoregressive step.</p>

<h3>Bidirectional attention enables parallel prediction</h3>
<p>Within a draft block, the diffusion model uses <strong>bidirectional attention</strong>: every masked position can attend to all other positions in the block. This is impossible in autoregressive generation (you can't attend to tokens you haven't predicted yet), but is natural for diffusion models where the entire block is denoised simultaneously.</p>

<p>Crucially, attention across blocks is <em>disabled</em>. Block b cannot attend to block b+1, preserving causal consistency: the draft model only uses information from the verified prefix and the current block.</p>
</section>

<!-- ── SECTION 05 ── -->
<section id="s-training" data-code="training">
<div class="section-no">05 — Training Innovations</div>
<h2>Four Training Tricks That Make the Difference</h2>
<p>Training a diffusion drafter that aligns with a frozen autoregressive target model requires several careful design choices. Standard block diffusion training produces a mediocre drafter; DFlash introduces four modifications that together push acceptance lengths from ~3× to 6×.</p>

<!-- Step navigator -->
<div class="step-list" id="train-steps">
  <div class="step-box active" data-step="0">
    <div class="step-num">1</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Random Anchor Sampling</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Instead of uniformly dividing responses into fixed blocks, randomly sample anchor tokens (clean tokens) from the response. Mask the following γ−1 positions as the prediction target. This exposes the draft model to diverse context features and matches inference-time behavior (where the anchor is always a verified clean token).</div>
      <div class="step-code">anchor = random.choice(response_tokens)
block = [anchor, &lt;m&gt;, &lt;m&gt;, ..., &lt;m&gt;]  # γ-1 masks</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box" data-step="1">
    <div class="step-num">2</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Exponential Loss Weighting</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Errors early in a draft block cascade: if token 2 is wrong, tokens 3–8 are automatically rejected. DFlash applies exponentially decaying weights: position k receives weight exp(−(k−1)/γ), strongly penalizing early errors and accelerating convergence to high acceptance lengths.</div>
      <div class="step-code">w_k = exp(-(k-1) / gamma)  # k in [1, gamma]
loss = sum(w_k * CE(pred_k, target_k))</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box" data-step="2">
    <div class="step-num">3</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Shared Embedding + LM Head</div>
      <div class="step-desc">The draft model shares the token embedding layer and language model head with the frozen target model. Only the 5 draft transformer layers are updated. This dramatically reduces trainable parameters and forces the draft model to operate in the target's representation space — acting as a lightweight diffusion adapter rather than an independent model.</div>
      <div class="step-code">draft.embed = target.embed   # frozen
draft.lm_head = target.lm_head  # frozen
# only draft.layers[0..4] are trained</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box" data-step="3">
    <div class="step-num">4</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Efficient Long-Context Training</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Long-context speculative decoding is expensive for EAGLE-3 (the training-time test tree grows with context). DFlash fixes the number of masked blocks per sequence regardless of length, and randomly samples anchor positions each epoch. This caps training cost while providing effective data augmentation over diverse positions in million-token contexts.</div>
      <div class="step-code">blocks_per_seq = 4   # fixed — not proportional to length
for epoch in range(N):
    anchors = sample_random(response, k=blocks_per_seq)</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="step-nav">
  <button id="train-prev" disabled>← prev</button>
  <span id="train-prog" style="font-size:.7rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">Step 1 of 4</span>
  <button id="train-next">next →</button>
</div>

<p>All four modifications together — random anchors, early-position weighting, shared embeddings, and fixed-block long-context training — are necessary. The paper's ablations show each contributes meaningfully: Table 9 in the paper shows random anchor sampling alone provides a significant boost over uniform block construction.</p>
</section>

<!-- ── SECTION 06 ── -->
<section id="s-mimo" data-code="mimo">
<div class="section-no">06 — MiMo 2.5 Pro: 1000 TPS</div>
<h2>Stacking the Stack: FP4 + DFlash + TileRT = 1000 TPS</h2>
<p>MiMo 2.5 Pro is a 1.02-trillion-parameter Mixture-of-Experts model. Getting it past 1000 tokens/second requires not one optimization, but three stacked together: aggressive quantization of the model itself, DFlash speculative decoding, and a new inference kernel that eliminates microsecond-scale execution gaps.</p>

<h3>Layer 1: MXFP4 Expert Quantization</h3>
<p>MoE models have a peculiar quantization profile: the expert layers hold over 95% of all parameters, but individual expert weights are accessed sparsely (only 2–8 experts activate per token). Xiaomi applies <strong>MXFP4 format</strong> (block size 32) selectively to MoE expert weights, while keeping attention projections and non-expert modules at higher precision. QAT (Quantization-Aware Training) restores capability to near-lossless levels.</p>

<table class="results-table" style="margin:1.25rem 0">
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Benchmark</th><th>FP8 baseline</th><th>MXFP4</th><th>Δ</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Claw-Eval pass³</td><td class="mn">63.8</td><td class="mn best">67.8</td><td class="mn" style="color:var(--green)">+6.3%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Humanity's Last Exam</td><td class="mn">48.0</td><td class="mn">47.0</td><td class="mn" style="color:rgba(239,68,68,.7)">−2.1%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>SWE-Bench Pro</td><td class="mn">57.2</td><td class="mn best">58.8</td><td class="mn" style="color:var(--green)">+2.8%</td></tr>
    <tr class="highlight-row"><td>SWE-bench Verified</td><td class="mn">78.9</td><td class="mn">77.4</td><td class="mn" style="color:rgba(239,68,68,.7)">−1.9%</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>The quality degradation is minimal: the MXFP4 model is "essentially on par" with the FP8 baseline, with some benchmarks actually improving due to the regularization effect of QAT.</p>

<h3>Layer 2: DFlash Drafter (MiMo-specific)</h3>
<p>The MiMo deployment uses DFlash with a 5-layer draft model capturing backbone layers at positions <a href="https://huggingface.co/XiaomiMiMo/MiMo-V2.5-Pro-FP4-DFlash/blob/main/dflash/config.json#L15" target="_blank" rel="noopener">[0, 15, 31, 47, 69]</a> (uniformly sampled across the 70-layer backbone). The draft uses <a href="https://huggingface.co/XiaomiMiMo/MiMo-V2.5-Pro-FP4-DFlash/blob/main/dflash/config.json#L20-L21" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong>Sliding Window Attention (SWA)</strong></a> with a 1024-token window, making per-prediction compute constant regardless of context length — critical for MiMo's 1M-token context support.</p>

<p>The drafter is trained with the <a href="https://huggingface.co/XiaomiMiMo/MiMo-V2.5-Pro-FP4-DFlash/blob/main/README.md#L95" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong>Muon second-order optimizer</strong></a> and model self-distillation, achieving acceptance lengths of 6.30 for coding tasks (out of a block size of 8). Each verification cycle thus delivers roughly 6× the tokens of a standard autoregressive step.</p>

<h3>Layer 3: TileRT Persistent Engine</h3>
<p>Even with DFlash delivering long acceptance chains, microsecond-scale gaps between kernel launches can add up. TileRT introduces two kernel-level innovations:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Persistent Engine Kernel</strong> — the entire decode pipeline stays resident in GPU registers; prefetching of the next block's weights continuously overlaps with computation, eliminating DRAM idle time</li>
  <li><strong>Warp Specialization</strong> — communication warps, data-movement warps, and tensor-compute warps are physically separated into distinct warp groups executing in parallel, enabling heterogeneous concurrency within a single kernel launch</li>
</ul>

